1
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:05,690
It's very expensive to take anything to the surface of the moon.

2
00:00:05,690 --> 00:00:11,290
So if we are able to reuse or recycle the materials we have there, then we will be able

3
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:16,490
to be much more sustainable and be able to stay there longer and do more work while we're

4
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,490
there.

5
00:00:17,490 --> 00:00:21,290
We're trying to maximize the value of the things that we send up there.

6
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,170
So if they're more recyclable and we can actually change them into something else

7
00:00:25,170 --> 00:00:28,770
that's usable, it's better economically to do it that way.

8
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:33,530
So just discarding items creates a safety hazard and a pollution hazard and we really

9
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:35,250
don't want that on the surface of the moon.

10
00:00:35,250 --> 00:00:39,570
It would be much more efficient and allow us to have a much more capable lunar surface

11
00:00:39,570 --> 00:00:43,330
moon base if we can reuse and recycle the materials.

12
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:47,610
This is where the game has changed for us with the moon base program is that it's

13
00:00:47,610 --> 00:00:52,010
no longer just a short-sighted view on what it means to bring people to the moon.

14
00:00:52,010 --> 00:00:56,090
We're really trying to set up an infrastructure that enables us to have a sustained presence

15
00:00:56,090 --> 00:01:00,290
and when you're doing this, it means that we must think about the end of mind.

16
00:01:00,290 --> 00:01:05,330
The lunar recycle challenge, it's ambitious because we are asking teams to actually create

17
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:10,730
prototypes and actually recycle the materials we are currently using and are planning on

18
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:13,850
using for some time in the foreseeable future.

19
00:01:13,850 --> 00:01:17,570
So there's just a lot of complexity to some of these materials and the teams

20
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:22,790
really took it upon themselves to find innovative ways to single out these materials and

21
00:01:22,790 --> 00:01:26,230
separate them and try to process them or give them another life.

22
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:30,210
The range of ideas was gigantic.

23
00:01:30,210 --> 00:01:34,830
They took materials that were in completely different material classes from plastics to

24
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:39,790
textiles to metals, all of which have different recycling methodologies and they were able

25
00:01:39,790 --> 00:01:43,270
to make very useful things with many different types of materials.

26
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:47,350
So a successfully cycling system for the moon needs to accomplish many things.

27
00:01:47,350 --> 00:01:54,350
In addition to recycling and creating products from the trash, it needs to be easy to use,

28
00:01:54,350 --> 00:01:59,470
it needs to be efficient to use, it needs to not use a lot of additional resources.

29
00:01:59,470 --> 00:02:02,790
In terms of energy or water, those are going to be hard to get.

30
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:07,550
It also needs to be safe for the crew and not use a lot of the crew's time.

31
00:02:07,550 --> 00:02:12,070
In this challenge, we wanted to go out to the world and ask the world to help

32
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:13,670
solve this problem.

33
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:17,070
Open innovation is extremely valuable to NASA.

34
00:02:17,070 --> 00:02:19,710
NASA is filled with extremely smart people.

35
00:02:19,710 --> 00:02:24,750
NASA has extremely smart partners that are all very good at what they do.

36
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:29,990
What open innovation does, however, is it broadens that solver pool.

37
00:02:29,990 --> 00:02:34,750
It broadens those ideas and it is something that really can augment what we're already

38
00:02:34,750 --> 00:02:39,310
doing to get a diversity of ideas in that you may not otherwise find.

39
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:42,870
Why find an e-bill in the haystack when you can have that needle find you?

40
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:50,750
And so it's been wonderful to be able to see the diverse ideas and the different modes

41
00:02:50,750 --> 00:02:55,270
that people take when they're thinking about how we go about recycling and it just speaks

42
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:59,950
to the power of the many when it comes to trying to get as many people thinking about

43
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:04,470
problems, whether it's in space, on earth, or anything in between.

44
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:15,660
Please join me in congratulating Keith Zerberst.

45
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:18,740
This has been such an amazing experience.

46
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:24,100
We compete in these challenges to learn, to grow, and to be a part of something exciting

47
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:28,180
and to be a part of what's happening right now in space and we just want to be a part

48
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:28,460
of it.

